## 9. Large file upload

### 9.1 The problem

The automatic sync described in section 2 moves a file through Apex: the code reads the
file out of Salesforce, then packages it for SharePoint. Apex has a fixed 12 MB working
memory allowance for background processing, and the file occupies that allowance twice —
once as it is read and once as it is packaged — after being converted to a text encoding
that adds roughly a third to its size.

A 6 MB document therefore needs around 16 MB of a 12 MB allowance. Files above
approximately 6 MB cannot be moved this way.

This is a Salesforce platform limit that applies to every customer. It is not affected by
how the code is written, and no configuration setting increases it.

Salesforce itself accepts attachments up to 2 GB, so a user can attach a document that the
sync cannot move. The `Max File Size Bytes` setting exists so that those files are
identified and recorded rather than failing with an unhelpful platform error.

### 9.2 The approach

The file is sent from the user's browser straight to SharePoint, without passing through
Apex at all. Apex is responsible only for the small requests at either end.

> **[Screenshot 15 — Sequence diagram]**
> *Insert a diagram with three lanes: Browser, Apex, SharePoint. Show Apex requesting an
> upload session, the URL being returned to the browser, the file travelling directly from
> browser to SharePoint, then the browser reporting the file id back to Apex. Emphasise
> that the file itself never crosses the Apex lane.*

The sequence:

1. The user selects a file using the **Upload to SharePoint** button on the Opportunity
   record page.
2. Apex asks SharePoint to open an upload session for that filename in that record's
   folder. This is a small JSON request; the file is not involved.
3. SharePoint returns a one-time upload address.
4. Apex passes that address to the browser.
5. The browser sends the file directly to SharePoint in 5 MB pieces. Each piece carries a
   byte range so SharePoint can reassemble the document.
6. The final piece returns the created document.
7. The browser tells Apex the document's identifier, and Apex completes the same follow-up
   work the automatic sync performs: writing the library columns, granting the record
   owner access, and recording the folder link on the Opportunity.

Because the file never occupies Apex memory, the 6 MB limit does not apply. Files of
50 MB and above upload successfully.

### 9.3 The upload address

The address returned in step 3 is not a general purpose credential. It has four properties
worth recording:

| Property | Detail |
|---|---|
| Pre-authenticated | Authorisation is embedded in the address. The browser attaches no token of its own, and the application's own credentials never leave the Salesforce server |
| Single destination | Bound to one document library, one folder, and one filename. It cannot be redirected to another location |
| Write only | Grants no ability to read any document, including the one being written |
| Short lived | Expires approximately 15 minutes after being issued |

What reaches the browser is therefore a time-limited permission to write one specific file
to one specific place, not access to the SharePoint site.

### 9.4 Transfer mechanism

Each piece is sent with a byte range identifying its position in the file. SharePoint
acknowledges each piece and states which range it expects next, returning the completed
document when the final piece arrives.

Two consequences follow from this design:

- **Progress is measurable.** The component displays a progress bar because it knows how
  many bytes have been acknowledged.
- **The transfer is resumable.** A dropped connection does not require the upload to
  restart from the beginning, because the session records which ranges have been received.

Piece size is 5 MB, which satisfies Microsoft's requirement that every piece except the
last be a multiple of 320 KiB.

### 9.5 The Salesforce copy

A file uploaded this way is stored in SharePoint. It is not stored in Salesforce, for the
same reason that gave rise to the feature: a file too large for Apex to read is also too
large for Apex to write.

To keep the user experience consistent, the integration adds a **link entry** to the
record's Files related list. Salesforce supports a link type file, which behaves like any
other file in the related list but points at an external address rather than holding
content. It has no size limit.

> **[Screenshot 16 — Files related list showing the link entry]**
> *Show the uploaded document appearing in the Opportunity's Files list, and note that
> opening it takes the user to SharePoint.*

The practical differences between a link entry and a stored file:

| | Stored file (automatic sync) | Link entry (large upload) |
|---|---|---|
| Size limit | Approximately 6 MB | None |
| Held in Salesforce | Yes | No |
| Preview in Salesforce | Yes | No — opens in SharePoint |
| Requires SharePoint access to open | No | Yes |
| Survives deletion in SharePoint | Yes | No — becomes a broken link |

This should be explained to users, because the two upload routes behave differently. See
the recommendation in section 9.8.

### 9.6 Components

| Type | Name | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Apex class | `SharePointLargeFileController` | Opens the upload session and performs the follow-up work |
| LWC | `sharepointFileViewer` | Upload button, progress bar, and the piece-by-piece transfer |
| Custom metadata field | `Create_Salesforce_Link__c` | Controls whether a link entry is added to Salesforce Files |

`SharePointLargeFileController` is a new class. No existing class was modified to add this
feature; the new class reuses the existing public methods for folder naming, column
writing, and permissions.

### 9.7 Configuration

One additional platform setting is required, because browsers block requests from a
Lightning page to an external address unless that address is explicitly permitted.

> **[Screenshot 17 — CSP Trusted Site]**
> *Setup → CSP Trusted Sites. Show the entry with connect-src enabled.*

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Trusted Site Name | `SharePoint_Upload` |
| URL | `https://bankunited.sharepoint.com` |
| Active | Yes |
| Context | All |
| CSP Directive | connect-src |

Without this entry the browser silently refuses the transfer, and the failure appears in
the browser console rather than as a Salesforce error.

One additional configuration field:

| Field | Value | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| Create Salesforce Link | Checked | Adds a link entry to the record's Files list after upload |

No change to the Entra ID application permissions is required. The upload session is
created with the same application permission already granted for the automatic sync.

### 9.8 Recommendation on user experience

The record page now offers two ways to add a document, with different behaviour:

1. The standard **Files** related list — the document is stored in Salesforce and copied to
   SharePoint automatically, up to approximately 6 MB.
2. The **Upload to SharePoint** button — any size, stored in SharePoint with a link entry
   in Salesforce.

Presenting two routes without explanation will cause confusion. Two options:

- **Label the distinction.** Rename the button to make the behaviour self-evident, for
  example "Upload large file to SharePoint". Lowest effort, and sufficient if the large
  file route is genuinely occasional.
- **Consolidate on one route.** Direct users to the component for all uploads, accepting
  that documents then live in SharePoint with a link in Salesforce regardless of size.
  Simpler to explain, at the cost of losing in-Salesforce preview for small documents.

This is a business decision rather than a technical one and should be confirmed with the
Wholesale Banking team before wider rollout.

### 9.9 Testing

| Area covered | Assertion |
|---|---|
| Upload session created | Session requested for the correct folder and filename |
| Existing document protected | Session requests a non-destructive conflict behaviour |
| Missing folder | Folder chain created and the session request retried |
| Follow-up work | Columns written with the file name and uploader email |
| Column failure | Reported as a warning; the document remains in SharePoint |
| Link entry | Created as a link type with no content stored in Salesforce |
| Loop prevention | The link entry does not trigger the automatic sync |
| Validation | Missing record id or document id is rejected |

